explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/further-education-and-skills/2024-25

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **SECTION 4**                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| **Industry-Specific Digital Skills**                                  |
|                                                                       |
| What general qualifications don\'t cover                              |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Specialist, sector-specific digital competencies employers expect but
general digital skills qualifications do not cover. Use this section
when working with Heads of Area to audit specific gaps and embed them in
digital hours within the Business Plan.

**Construction & Built Environment**

**BUI · CON · ENG · EHE**

**BIM & CAD Software**

Revit, AutoCAD --- reading, producing, and annotating digital drawings.
BIM Level 2 is now a UK government procurement requirement for
contractors.

**Digital Site Management**

QR clock-in, digital site diaries, health & safety reporting apps
(Fieldwire, Procore, Site App Pro). Standard on all major contract
sites.

**Digital Measuring & Surveying Devices**

Laser levels, digital theodolites, drone survey tools --- reading and
digitally logging measurement outputs.

**Invoicing & Materials Ordering**

Sage, Xero, trade supplier portals --- raising purchase orders and
tracking digital delivery notes.

**Sustainability & Carbon Calculation Tools**

BREEAM digital tools, energy modelling software, materials calculators
for net-zero construction standards.

**Health, Care & Early Years**

**HAC · EEY · FEH**

**Electronic Patient / Care Record Systems**

SystmOne, EMIS, Liquid Logic --- navigating digital care records
securely and accurately. Mandatory in NHS and care settings.

**Telehealth & Remote Care Platforms**

Video consultation platforms, wearable health tech interpretation,
remote monitoring tools. Growing post-pandemic standard.

**Digital Observation & Portfolio (EEY)**

Tapestry, Evidence Me --- digital child observation recording, EYFS
tracking, parent communication platforms.

**GDPR & Data Security in Care Contexts**

Caldicott principles, NHS data handling compliance, safe digital record
management. Legal requirement for all care staff.

**e-Prescribing & Medication Management**

Electronic prescribing systems, digital MAR charts, medication
administration recording software.

**Engineering & Motor Vehicle**

**EMV · EFE · ENG · EHE**

**Vehicle Diagnostic Systems**

OBD II scanners, manufacturer-specific software (Bosch, ALLDATA), EV
battery management systems. Modern vehicles cannot be diagnosed without
digital tools.

**CAD / CAM Systems**

SolidWorks, Fusion 360, CNC programming interfaces --- from digital
design to digitally controlled manufacture.

**Industrial IoT & Sensor Data**

Reading and acting on data from IoT-connected machines; predictive
maintenance platforms; digital quality monitoring.

**ADAS Calibration Tools**

Advanced Driver Assistance Systems calibration --- critical new skill
for all modern vehicle technicians post-2023.

**ERP / MRP Systems**

Enterprise resource planning for manufacturing scheduling, materials
management, and production tracking (SAP, Oracle).

**Hair, Beauty & Hospitality**

**HBH**

**Salon & Venue Booking Platforms**

Fresha, Treatwell, Vagaro (beauty) / OpenTable, ResDiary (hospitality)
--- automated booking, client records, deposit management.

**EPOS / POS Systems**

Electronic point of sale --- order processing, end-of-day
reconciliation, stock management. Used in all commercial hospitality
settings.

**Allergen & Food Safety Digital Systems**

Natasha\'s Law compliance software, digital HACCP records, temperature
logging apps. Legal requirement for all food businesses.

**HMRC Making Tax Digital (Self-Employed)**

Self-assessment, VAT digital submission, income tracking for
self-employed beauty professionals. Mandatory from 2026.

**Social Media as Business Development**

Instagram and TikTok as professional portfolio and client acquisition
platforms --- now core to salon marketing strategy.

**Creative Arts, Games & Media**

**AGF · GMA · PAP**

**Adobe Creative Suite**

Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, Premiere, After Effects --- the
industry-standard toolkit. Not knowing Adobe CC is not an option in
creative employment.

**Game Engines (Unity / Unreal)**

Real-time rendering, asset optimisation, interactive media production.
Essential for GMA learners entering the UK games industry.

**AI-Assisted Creative Tools**

Midjourney, Adobe Firefly, Suno --- ethical use in creative production
with critical evaluation of intellectual property implications.

**Digital Rights & IP Management**

Copyright, Creative Commons licensing, protecting original creative
output, understanding platform terms of service.

**Digital Production Pipelines**

Version control (Git/Perforce), cloud collaboration, sprint-based
workflows. How professional creative studios actually operate.

**Public Services & Protective Services**

**PSF**

**Police / Emergency Service Digital Systems**

Niche RMS (police records), Airwave / digital radio systems, body-worn
camera evidence management and data chain of custody.

**Digital Mapping & GIS**

Geographic information systems for operational planning --- used across
fire, police, and military roles.

**OSINT Awareness**

Open-source intelligence tools --- online research skills for
investigative contexts, with ethical and legal grounding.

**Digital Report Writing & Evidence Documentation**

Court-ready digital documentation, official report formatting, digital
chain-of-custody protocols.

**Sport & Active Living**

**SPO**

**Performance Analysis Software**

Hudl, Dartfish, Catapult --- video tagging, GPS data analysis, match
analysis reporting. Used by all professional clubs.

**Wearable Tech Data Interpretation**

Heart rate, GPS, load monitoring from Garmin, Polar, Catapult ---
interpreting data for training prescription and injury prevention.

**Online Coaching & Client Platforms**

Trainerize, PT Distinction, My PT Hub --- digital client management and
remote coaching delivery for self-employed coaches.

**Business, Digital & IT**

**BUS · DCI**

**Accounting & Finance Platforms**

Xero, QuickBooks, Sage --- bookkeeping, VAT returns, payroll. HMRC
Making Tax Digital compliance is now a legal requirement.

**CRM & Marketing Automation**

Salesforce, HubSpot, Mailchimp --- customer management, pipeline
tracking, automated digital marketing campaigns.

**Cloud Platforms & DevOps (DCI)**

Microsoft Azure, AWS, GitHub --- cloud infrastructure, version control,
deployment pipelines for computing learners.

**Cybersecurity Tools (DCI)**

Ethical hacking tools (Wireshark, Kali Linux), CISSP frameworks,
penetration testing basics --- for computing and digital specialism
learners.

**Sources**

WEF Future of Jobs 2025:
weforum.org/stories/2025/01/future-of-jobs-report-2025

FutureDotNow AI-Ready Workforce (Oct 2025):
futuredotnow.uk/new-report-charts-path-to-ai-ready-uk-workforce/

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **SECTION 5**                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| **Framing Digital Skills**                                            |
|                                                                       |
| For Industry, Vocational & Work Contexts                              |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

How digital skills are framed determines whether staff and learners
engage or disengage. The most effective framing connects a digital skill
directly to an industry outcome learners already care about. Each
approach below is paired with concrete examples from Weston College
curriculum areas.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **FRAMING APPROACH 1**                                                |
|                                                                       |
| **The Industry Standard Frame**                                       |
|                                                                       |
| Frame digital skills as standard professional practice in that sector |
| --- not as technology training, but as what every employer expects on |
| day one.                                                              |
|                                                                       |
| **Worked examples:**                                                  |
|                                                                       |
| -   Construction (CON/BUI): Instead of \'Today we\'re learning about  |
|     QR codes\', say: \'Every major contractor uses digital clock-in.  |
|     You won\'t get paid if you can\'t sign in on site digitally.\'    |
|                                                                       |
| -   Engineering (EMV): Instead of \'Digital diagnostics session\',    |
|     say: \'EV diagnostics are entirely digital --- you cannot fix a   |
|     modern vehicle without a tablet.\'                                |
|                                                                       |
| -   Health (HAC): Instead of \'Electronic records lesson\', say:      |
|     \'Every NHS and care setting uses digital records. Paper records  |
|     no longer exist in most settings.\'                               |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **FRAMING APPROACH 2**                                                |
|                                                                       |
| **The Time-Saving Frame**                                             |
|                                                                       |
| Frame digital tools as reducing admin burden for tasks learners will  |
| have to do anyway --- invoicing, booking, recording, communicating.   |
|                                                                       |
| **Worked examples:**                                                  |
|                                                                       |
| -   Hair & Beauty (HBH): \'Fresha sends reminders automatically and   |
|     takes deposits. You spend less time chasing no-shows and more     |
|     time with clients.\'                                              |
|                                                                       |
| -   Health & Care (HAC): \'Electronic records mean you don\'t rewrite |
|     the same patient information by hand three times a shift.\'       |
|                                                                       |
| -   Hospitality (HBH): \'EPOS means end-of-day reconciliation takes 5 |
|     minutes instead of an hour.\'                                     |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **FRAMING APPROACH 3**                                                |
|                                                                       |
| **The Employability & Earnings Frame**                                |
|                                                                       |
| Link digital competence directly to higher pay, better job prospects, |
| or self-employment viability. Use Labour Market Intelligence to make  |
| the case concrete.                                                    |
|                                                                       |
| **Worked examples:**                                                  |
|                                                                       |
| -   Business (BUS): \'Xero certification adds £3-5k to your starting  |
|     salary as a bookkeeper or finance assistant.\'                    |
|                                                                       |
| -   Performing Arts (PAP): \'Casting directors now expect digital     |
|     self-tapes. You won\'t get seen without knowing how to film,      |
|     edit, and upload professionally.\'                                |
|                                                                       |
| -   Construction (CON): \'Site managers who can use BIM earn £10-15k  |
|     more than those who can\'t.\'                                     |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **FRAMING APPROACH 4**                                                |
|                                                                       |
| **The Authentic Context Frame**                                       |
|                                                                       |
| Teach digital skills inside real vocational tasks rather than as      |
| separate lessons. The digital skill becomes invisible --- the learner |
| is just doing their job.                                              |
|                                                                       |
| **Worked examples:**                                                  |
|                                                                       |
| -   Construction (CON): \'Measure this wall, log the data, and email  |
|     it to the project manager.\' The learner has practised data       |
|     entry, file handling, and professional email in one task.         |
|                                                                       |
| -   ESOL (ESO): \'Book a GP appointment on the NHS App.\' The learner |
|     is navigating a real digital service --- not a classroom          |
|     simulation.                                                       |
|                                                                       |
| -   Sport (SPO): \'Record today\'s session data in Hudl and share the |
|     analysis with the team.\'                                         |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**THE GOLDEN RULE OF INDUSTRY FRAMING**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| Never teach the technology. Teach the task that technology enables.   |
|                                                                       |
| A construction learner doesn\'t need to learn \'how to use an app.\'  |
| They need to clock in, submit a site diary, order materials, and read |
| a drawing. The apps are just how they do it. When digital skills are  |
| framed this way --- across every curriculum area --- they stop being  |
| a separate burden and become invisible, purposeful, and               |
| self-evidently necessary.                                             |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**Sources**

FutureDotNow EDS Framework & Framing Guidance:
futuredotnow.uk/about-us/the-essential-digital-skills-framework/

Good Things Foundation --- Digital Inclusion:
goodthingsfoundation.org/policy-and-research/what-works-colab/basic-digital-skills-briefing

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **SECTION 6**                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| **SEND, Inclusion & Accessibility**                                   |
|                                                                       |
| Weston College SEND Centre of Excellence                              |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Weston College is one of three SEND Centres for Excellence in the UK.
Digital skills development for learners with SEND requires a
fundamentally different approach --- technology is not just a learning
tool, it is often the means by which learning is possible at all.

**FIP Autism (FAU)**

-   Many autistic learners have strong digital aptitude --- leverage
    this as a recognised strength in ILPs and coaching conversations.

-   Predictable, consistent digital environments reduce anxiety ---
    maintain consistent Teams layout, clear file naming conventions, and
    avoid sudden platform changes without notice.

-   AAC apps (Proloquo2Go, Grid 3) support communication in digital
    learning contexts --- ensure all staff know what is in use.

-   Visual schedule apps (Choiceworks) support session structure and
    transitions digitally.

-   Sensory considerations --- screen brightness, notification sounds,
    and visual clutter in digital tools can cause distress.

**FIP Complex Needs (FCO)**

-   Eye-gaze technology (Tobii) enables digital access for learners
    without motor control --- this is communication, not optional.

-   Switch-access devices provide alternative computer interaction ---
    staff must be trained to support this.

-   Clicker / Widgit Online for literacy and communication support ---
    available on all college devices.

-   Technology is often the primary communication channel for this
    cohort --- reliability of devices and software is critical, not
    peripheral.

-   Staff training on assistive technology is non-negotiable, not
    optional CPD.

**FIP Pre-Vocational (FPL)**

-   Basic device operation as a foundation skill for transition to
    employment --- digital daily living tools (travel apps, banking
    apps, NHS App) support transition.

-   Read&Write (text-to-speech, dictation) reduces writing barriers for
    learners with literacy difficulties.

-   Immersive Reader (built into Microsoft Teams and Word) --- free,
    effective, and should be enabled by default.

-   Taskmaster / visual timetable apps support daily digital routines in
    the classroom and at home.

**ESOL Learners (ESO)**

-   Compound barrier: language AND digital exclusion simultaneously ---
    both must be addressed, not sequenced.

-   Microsoft Translator and Google Translate as scaffolding tools ---
    bridges to independence, not dependencies.

-   Real-world digital tasks (NHS App, DWP portal, online banking) are
    most motivating and most immediately useful.

-   DfE FE Stats Q1 2025/26: ESOL is the most popular essential skills
    subject (118,220 learners) --- digital provision must scale with
    this demand.

**Dyslexia & SpLD (Across All Areas)**

-   Read&Write Gold for text-to-speech, word prediction, and screen
    masking --- available under college licence.

-   Immersive Reader built into Microsoft Teams and Word --- zero cost,
    high impact.

-   OneNote digital inking for spatial note-taking --- particularly
    effective for learners who process information visually.

-   Dragon Dictate / speech-to-text for extended writing tasks ---
    reduces the cognitive load of composition.

**Universal Design for Learning (All Areas)**

-   WCAG 2.2 AA compliance for all digital resources created by staff
    --- w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/

-   Captions on all video content --- use Microsoft Teams
    auto-captioning and check accuracy before sharing.

-   Alt-text on all images in presentations and SharePoint pages --- two
    sentences maximum, descriptive.

-   Accessible colour contrast in PowerPoints --- minimum 4.5:1 ratio
    for normal text, 3:1 for large text.

-   Microsoft Office Accessibility Checker --- build checking into staff
    CPD as a professional habit, not a one-off.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Key principle: Accessibility is not a bolt-on. The best digital
  accessibility practice benefits all learners --- captions help in noisy
  environments, clear navigation helps anxious learners, and accessible
  colour contrast helps in poor lighting conditions. Build it in from the
  start.
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Sources**

WCAG 2.2 Quick Reference (W3C): w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/

Gov.UK Accessibility Requirements for Public Sector:
gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps

TextHelp Read&Write for Education:
texthelp.com/en-gb/products/read-write/

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **SECTION 7**                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| **Digital Skills Progression**                                        |
|                                                                       |
| Foundation to Highly Advanced · DigComp 3.0 (Nov 2025)                |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Two parallel progression ladders structured using DigComp 3.0
proficiency levels (European Commission, November 2025). Foundation,
Basic, Intermediate, Advanced, and Highly Advanced. Use these to
position learners and staff on a shared scale for CPD planning, digital
hours evidence, and self-assessment.

DigComp 3.0 (Nov 2025): joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu --- navigate
to Projects \> Education & Training \> DigComp 3.0

**Ladder 1: General Digital Skills**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **FOUNDATION LEVEL · GUIDED, ROUTINE TASKS IN FAMILIAR CONTEXTS**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Switching on & basic navigation**

Can switch on a device, use a mouse or touchscreen, and open an app or
browser. Needs guidance for most tasks.

**Recognising common icons & interfaces**

Identifies Wi-Fi, battery, and settings icons. Can tap familiar apps but
cannot transfer skills to new interfaces independently.

**Basic internet browsing**

Can open a browser, type a URL or search term. May not distinguish
between search results and adverts.

**Sending and receiving basic messages**

Can send a text or WhatsApp message. Struggles with email attachments or
creating contacts from scratch.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **BASIC LEVEL · WELL-DEFINED TASKS IN FAMILIAR CONTEXTS, SOME
  INDEPENDENCE**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Email --- composing, replying, attaching**

Can write, send, reply to, and forward emails with attachments. Knows
not to open suspicious attachments.

**Searching effectively online**

Uses specific multi-word search terms. Opens multiple tabs, bookmarks
pages. Begins to question reliability of sources.

**Creating simple documents**

Can open Word or Google Docs, type, format (bold, bullet lists, font
size), save, and retrieve files from a folder.

**Basic online transactions**

Can shop online, fill in a digital form, create an account, use online
banking to view a balance.

**Accessing public digital services**

Can navigate Gov.UK, book a GP appointment online, use the NHS App
independently.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **INTERMEDIATE LEVEL · WELL-DEFINED TASKS INDEPENDENTLY, ADAPTS TO
  COMMON SITUATIONS**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Productive use of office applications**

Uses Word, Excel (formulae, sorting, charts), and PowerPoint
confidently. Manages files across cloud storage.

**Video calling and digital collaboration**

Joins and hosts video calls (Teams, Zoom). Uses shared documents
simultaneously with others.

**Online safety --- active practice**

Uses unique strong passwords and a password manager. Recognises phishing
emails. Enables two-factor authentication.

**Evaluating digital information critically**

Checks publication dates, author credentials, and cross-references
sources. Recognises misinformation patterns.

**Creating and sharing digital content**

Produces presentations, short videos, infographics, or social posts for
a defined purpose and audience.

**Basic AI tool use**

Can use AI assistants (ChatGPT, Copilot) to draft text, summarise
documents, or answer questions. Knows to verify outputs.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **ADVANCED LEVEL · COMPLEX TASKS, ADAPTS ACROSS CONTEXTS, GUIDES
  OTHERS**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Advanced data management & analysis**

Excel pivot tables, VLOOKUP, IF statements. Basic Power BI dashboards.
Manages and cleans datasets.

**Digital project management**

Uses Planner, Trello, or Asana. Manages workflows, assigns tasks, tracks
progress, and reports on outcomes.

**AI as a strategic working tool**

Uses AI to accelerate work --- writing, research, summarising. Evaluates
outputs critically, writes effective prompts.

**Cybersecurity --- personal and organisational**

Understands GDPR obligations, recognises social engineering attacks,
manages security settings across devices.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **HIGHLY ADVANCED LEVEL · SOLVES HIGHLY COMPLEX PROBLEMS, CREATES NEW
  SOLUTIONS, LEADS OTHERS**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Strategic digital leadership**

Shapes digital culture, policy, or strategy at organisational or sector
level. Advocates for digital inclusion.

**Building and automating digital workflows**

Uses low-code / no-code tools (Power Automate, Zapier, Airtable) to
automate routine tasks.

**Advanced AI and digital innovation**

Identifies novel applications of AI. Builds custom AI workflows.
Evaluates emerging technologies for organisational use.

**Digital rights, ethics and advocacy**

Understands algorithmic bias, data sovereignty, and digital rights.
Advocates for ethical and inclusive digital practice.

**Ladder 2: IT Skills for FE & HE**

Based on: Jisc Digital Capabilities Framework ---
jisc.ac.uk/guides/digital-capabilities and ETF Digital Teaching
Professional Framework

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **FOUNDATION LEVEL · COLLEGE SYSTEM ACCESS WITH GUIDANCE**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Logging into college systems**

Can log in to college email, Teams, and the VLE. Needs prompting to
reset passwords.

**Accessing course materials online**

Can find and open resources posted by staff on the VLE or Teams channel
with guidance.

**Basic printing and scanning**

Can print from a college PC and use a flatbed scanner with guidance.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **BASIC LEVEL · CORE COLLEGE DIGITAL SYSTEMS USED INDEPENDENTLY**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**VLE navigation --- learner level**

Accesses assignment briefs, submission portals, grades, and feedback.
Submits work digitally.

**Microsoft 365 / Google Workspace basics**

Uses college email, Word/Docs, PowerPoint. Saves to OneDrive/Drive and
shares a document with a link.

**Submitting and referencing work digitally**

Submits via Turnitin, Teams assignment, or VLE portal. Uses a
referencing tool to format digital sources correctly.

**Using Microsoft Teams for study**

Joins class channels, participates in chats, accesses shared files,
attends online sessions.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **INTERMEDIATE LEVEL · PRODUCTIVE DIGITAL ACADEMIC PRACTICE**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Digital research & academic databases**

Uses college-licensed databases (EBSCO, JSTOR, ProQuest) with Boolean
search. Exports references to Zotero.

**Creating accessible digital presentations**

Produces clear presentations with accessible fonts, colour contrast, and
alt-text on images.

**Data handling for academic work**

Uses Excel or SPSS for basic analysis --- sorting, filtering, charts,
calculating means/percentages.

**ePortfolio and digital evidence management**

Builds and maintains a digital portfolio (Smart Assessor, Mahara,
OneNote). Responds to digital feedback.

**AI tools for study --- ethical use**

Uses Copilot, ChatGPT, or Grammarly with full understanding of academic
integrity and institutional AI policy.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **ADVANCED LEVEL · COMPLEX ACADEMIC DIGITAL PRACTICE, TRAINS OTHERS**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Advanced data analysis & visualisation**

Uses SPSS, R, or Python for statistical analysis. Produces
publication-quality charts. Briefs others on findings.

**Digital content production**

Produces multimedia academic content --- video essays, podcasts,
interactive documents, H5P resources.

**Digital professional identity & networking**

Maintains an active professional LinkedIn profile. Builds a digital
portfolio for employability.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **HIGHLY ADVANCED LEVEL · SCHOLARSHIP, INNOVATION, AND DIGITAL
  LEADERSHIP**
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Digital scholarship & open education**

Publishes research digitally (open access repositories, academic blogs).
Creates OERs for others.

**Computational thinking & automation**

Applies programming or scripting logic to academic/professional
problems. Automates repetitive tasks.

**Digital ethics leadership**

Advocates for responsible, inclusive digital practice. Contributes to
institutional or sector policy on AI, data, or digital access.

**Sources**

DigComp 3.0 --- EU Digital Competence Framework (Nov 2025):
joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu --- navigate to Projects \> Education
& Training \> DigComp 3.0

Jisc Digital Capabilities Framework:
jisc.ac.uk/guides/digital-capabilities

DfE Essential Digital Skills Framework:
gov.uk/government/publications/essential-digital-skills-framework

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **SECTION 8**                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| **Teacher Digital Skills**                                            |
|                                                                       |
| TLA-Focused Progression · ETF DTPF & DigCompEdu                       |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

A structured progression framework for FE & HE teaching staff --- from
first engaging with technology in the classroom to leading digital
innovation across the college. Built on three frameworks: the ETF
Digital Teaching Professional Framework (DTPF) --- the definitive
FE-specific teacher digital framework; DigCompEdu (A1--C2 progression
levels); and the ETF Professional Standards for FE Teachers.

ETF DTPF: et-foundation.co.uk --- Professional Development \> EdTech
Support \> Digital Skills Competency Framework

DigCompEdu: joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu/digcompedu_en

ETF Enhance Platform (free CPD): enhance.etfoundation.co.uk

**Overview --- The Three Development Stages**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **STAGE**         **ETF DTPF**      **WHAT IT LOOKS   **DIGCOMPEDU**
                                      LIKE**            
  ----------------- ----------------- ----------------- -----------------
  **Stage 1**       **Exploring**     Practitioner is   A1--A2 (Newcomer
                                      beginning to      → Explorer)
                                      explore digital   
                                      tools.            
                                      Assimilates new   
                                      information.      
                                      Relies on         
                                      guidance.         

  **Stage 2A**      **Adopting:       Applies digital   B1 (Integrator)
                    Developing**      practices in the  
                                      classroom and     
                                      begins expanding  
                                      them. Independent 
                                      in core tools.    

  **Stage 2B**      **Adopting:       Expands and       B2 (Expert)
                    Embedding**       deepens digital   
                                      practice. Designs 
                                      learning          
                                      experiences where 
                                      technology has    
                                      pedagogical       
                                      purpose.          

  **Stage 3**       **Leading**       Passes on         C1--C2 (Leader →
                                      knowledge,        Pioneer)
                                      critiques         
                                      existing          
                                      practice,         
                                      develops new      
                                      digital           
                                      approaches, and   
                                      leads others.     
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**Stage 1 --- Exploring Digital Teaching**

**DIGCOMPEDU A1--A2 · ETF DTPF STAGE 1 · TARGET: NEWLY APPOINTED STAFF,
CAREER CHANGERS, RETURNING STAFF**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **1.1 Accessing and using the VLE / LMS** · Professional Engagement   |
|                                                                       |
| Can log into the college VLE, view existing course content, and       |
| upload a simple resource. Understands how learners access the same    |
| space. Knows who to contact for VLE support.                          |
|                                                                       |
| Source: ETF DTPF Stage 1: et-foundation.co.uk → Professional          |
| Development → EdTech Support                                          |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **1.2 Using Microsoft Teams for communication** · Digital             |
| Communication                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| Can join a Teams meeting, post to a channel, share a file, and        |
| message a learner or colleague. Does not yet use advanced features    |
| like breakout rooms or Forms.                                         |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Microsoft Teams Help: support.microsoft.com/en-us/teams       |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **1.3 Creating basic digital presentations** · Digital Resources      |
|                                                                       |
| Produces PowerPoint slides with text and images. Understands the      |
| difference between a teaching slide deck and one shared with learners |
| online. Aware of legibility but not yet applying formal accessibility |
| standards.                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Microsoft PowerPoint accessibility: support.microsoft.com →   |
| search \'accessible presentations\'                                   |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **1.4 Submitting admin data digitally** · Professional Engagement     |
|                                                                       |
| Can complete register, funding, and compliance forms on college MIS   |
| systems (ProMonitor, e-ILP). Understands GDPR basics --- does not     |
| share learner data inappropriately.                                   |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Gov.UK Data Protection in Education:                          |
| gov.uk/guidance/data-protection-in-schools                            |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **1.5 Awareness of available digital tools** · Digital Awareness      |
|                                                                       |
| Has attended an overview of the college\'s key digital tools (Teams,  |
| VLE, Microsoft 365). Knows which tools are available and approved.    |
| Has not yet integrated them meaningfully into regular teaching.       |
|                                                                       |
| Source: ETF Enhance Platform: enhance.etfoundation.co.uk              |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**Stage 2A --- Adopting: Developing Digital Practice**

**DIGCOMPEDU B1 (INTEGRATOR) · ETF DTPF STAGE 2 · TARGET: STAFF WITH
1--2 YEARS EXPERIENCE, POST-ITE, INCONSISTENT DIGITAL TOOL USERS**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2.1 Structuring an active VLE course space** · Digital Resources    |
|                                                                       |
| Has built a well-organised VLE course with clear sections, resources  |
| for each topic, and learner-facing instructions. Learners can         |
| navigate independently. Content is current --- not a one-off upload   |
| at the start of term.                                                 |
|                                                                       |
| Source: ETF DTPF Stage 2: et-foundation.co.uk → Professional          |
| Development → EdTech Support → Digital Skills Competency Framework    |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2.2 Using Teams for active learner engagement** · Teaching &        |
| Learning                                                              |
|                                                                       |
| Uses Teams channels for ongoing learner discussion, Q&A, and          |
| feedback. Sets Teams assignments with rubrics and due dates. Uses     |
| breakout rooms for small-group work. Embeds Microsoft Forms for polls |
| or knowledge checks.                                                  |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Microsoft Teams for Learning: support.microsoft.com → search  |
| \'Teams for remote learning\'                                         |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2.3 Formative digital assessment** · Assessment                     |
|                                                                       |
| Uses digital tools for formative assessment --- Microsoft Forms       |
| quizzes, Mentimeter, Kahoot, or Padlet. Reviews response data to      |
| adapt teaching. Gives digital feedback on assignments via Teams or    |
| the VLE.                                                              |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Mentimeter for Education: mentimeter.com/features/education   |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2.4 Creating accessible digital resources** · Accessibility &       |
| Inclusion                                                             |
|                                                                       |
| Applies accessibility principles to all digital materials ---         |
| readable fonts (minimum 18pt), sufficient colour contrast, alt-text   |
| on images, headings in Word documents. Uses Microsoft Office          |
| Accessibility Checker. Understands WCAG 2.2 AA as the standard.       |
|                                                                       |
| Source: WCAG 2.2 Reference: w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/               |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2.5 Using data from college systems to inform teaching** ·          |
| Data-Informed Practice                                                |
|                                                                       |
| Reviews attendance, attainment, and target data from ProMonitor.      |
| Identifies at-risk learners digitally and records intervention. Uses  |
| VLE engagement data to understand learner participation.              |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Gov.UK Data in FE:                                            |
| gov.uk/guidance/using-data-effectively-in-further-education           |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2.6 Ethical AI awareness in TLA** · AI Literacy                     |
|                                                                       |
| Understands how learners are using AI tools (ChatGPT, Copilot) in     |
| their work. Knows the college\'s AI policy. Can identify likely       |
| AI-generated content. Begins using AI to reduce own workload (quiz    |
| generation, lesson plan drafts).                                      |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Jisc AI in Tertiary Education:                                |
| jisc.ac.uk/ai-in-tertiary-education                                   |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**Stage 2B --- Adopting: Embedding & Expanding**

**DIGCOMPEDU B2 (EXPERT) · ETF DTPF STAGE 2 (UPPER) · TARGET: CONFIDENT
PRACTITIONERS, THOSE SEEKING ATS OR DIGITAL CHAMPION STATUS**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2B.1 Technology-enhanced active learning design** · Pedagogical     |
| Innovation                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| Designs lesson sequences where digital tools purposefully change the  |
| learning experience --- enabling collaboration, problem-solving, or   |
| creative production, not just presenting content. Applies 21CLD or    |
| UDL frameworks to technology choices.                                 |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Microsoft 21CLD: microsoft.com/en-us/education → Teams →      |
| 21CLD · UDL Guidelines: udlguidelines.cast.org                        |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2B.2 Digital differentiation** · Learner Support                    |
|                                                                       |
| Uses digital tools to personalise learning --- adaptive quiz tools    |
| routing learners to different content, digital choice boards,         |
| differentiated resources shared via the VLE for different ability     |
| groups without additional printing.                                   |
|                                                                       |
| Source: DigCompEdu Area 4 --- Learner Support:                        |
| joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu/digcompedu_en                      |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2B.3 Producing and editing digital media for teaching** · Digital   |
| Content Creation                                                      |
|                                                                       |
| Produces screencasts (Loom, OBS, Teams recording), short              |
| instructional videos, or narrated PowerPoints for flipped learning.   |
| Captions all video content. Embeds media in the VLE rather than       |
| emailing links.                                                       |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Loom for Education: loom.com/education · Microsoft Stream     |
| captions: support.microsoft.com → search \'add captions\'             |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2B.4 Advanced digital assessment and feedback** · Assessment        |
|                                                                       |
| Uses Turnitin originality reports and rubrics. Provides audio or      |
| video feedback on learner work. Uses digital annotation tools on      |
| submitted PDFs. Designs digital summative assessments valid and       |
| cheat-resistant in an AI context.                                     |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Turnitin Feedback Studio:                                     |
| turnitin.com/solutions/feedback-studio                                |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2B.5 AI as a teaching productivity tool** · AI in TLA               |
|                                                                       |
| Uses AI tools (Copilot, ChatGPT, MagicSchool.ai, Eduaide.ai) to draft |
| lesson plans, generate differentiated resources, produce quiz banks,  |
| and summarise learner feedback. Critically evaluates AI outputs       |
| before use.                                                           |
|                                                                       |
| Source: MagicSchool AI: magicschool.ai · Jisc AI Guidance:            |
| jisc.ac.uk/ai-in-tertiary-education                                   |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **2B.6 Building learners\' digital skills in subject context** ·      |
| Learner Digital Empowerment                                           |
|                                                                       |
| Deliberately plans for learners to develop digital skills within the  |
| subject --- embedded in vocational tasks, not bolted on. Can          |
| articulate which EDS categories their learners are developing and how |
| this is evidenced.                                                    |
|                                                                       |
| Source: DfE EDS Framework:                                            |
| gov.uk/government/publications/essential-digital-skills-framework     |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

**Stage 3 --- Leading Digital Teaching Practice**

**DIGCOMPEDU C1--C2 (LEADER → PIONEER) · ETF DTPF STAGE 3 · TARGET:
DIGITAL CHAMPIONS, TLAMS WITH DIGITAL REMIT, STAFF PURSUING ATS**

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **3.1 Mentoring and coaching digital practice** · Professional        |
| Leadership                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| Supports and coaches colleagues on digital tools and pedagogy.        |
| Identifies where a colleague is on the DTPF progression and tailors   |
| coaching accordingly. Runs team CPD sessions with pedagogical         |
| rationale, not just \'how to\' instruction.                           |
|                                                                       |
| Source: ETF DTPF Stage 3: et-foundation.co.uk → Professional          |
| Development → EdTech Support → Digital Skills Competency Framework    |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **3.2 Designing digital-first curriculum** · Curriculum Design        |
|                                                                       |
| Designs entire units or programmes where digital tools are integral   |
| to the learning journey. The VLE is a live, learner-directed          |
| environment. Assessment strategy incorporates digital evidence mapped |
| to the EDS Framework.                                                 |
|                                                                       |
| Source: DigCompEdu Area 3 --- Teaching & Learning:                    |
| joint-research-centre.ec.europa.eu/digcompedu_en                      |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **3.3 Critical evaluation and adoption of EdTech** · Strategic        |
| Digital Leadership                                                    |
|                                                                       |
| Evaluates new educational technology critically --- pedagogical       |
| rationale, accessibility compliance, data privacy (GDPR), cost, and   |
| sustainability. Makes evidence-based recommendations on EdTech        |
| adoption or discontinuation.                                          |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Jisc Evaluating EdTech: jisc.ac.uk/guides/evaluating-edtech   |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **3.4 AI pedagogy leadership** · AI in Education                      |
|                                                                       |
| Leads the team\'s thinking on AI in teaching and assessment. Drafts   |
| or contributes to the area\'s AI policy. Supports colleagues in       |
| navigating the AI landscape. Stays current through professional       |
| reading and networks.                                                 |
|                                                                       |
| Source: Jisc AI Framework: jisc.ac.uk/ai-in-tertiary-education ·      |
| FutureDotNow AI-Ready Report: futuredotnow.uk                         |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **3.5 Digital research and innovation in practice** · Scholarship &   |
| Innovation                                                            |
|                                                                       |
| Conducts or participates in action research into digital pedagogy.    |
| Writes up or presents findings. Contributes to the evidence base on   |
| what works in digital teaching in FE & HE.                            |
|                                                                       |
| Source: ETF Research: et-foundation.co.uk/research/                   |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
| **3.6 Systemic digital inclusion advocacy** · Inclusion &             |
| Accessibility                                                         |
|                                                                       |
| Ensures digital accessibility is embedded at curriculum and resource  |
| level across the team. Audits the team\'s VLE provision for WCAG      |
| compliance. Champions assistive technology for SEND learners. Makes   |
| the case to leadership for resources that reduce digital exclusion.   |
|                                                                       |
| Source: WCAG 2.2: w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/                         |
+=======================================================================+
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

